      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Academic literature often characterizes the stability brought by newcomers as contingent on capital aggregation, which can outpace the organic cultural resilience of long-term residents. While the \"new\" demographic is frequently portrayed as injecting economic vitality, their contributions are sometimes critiqued for prioritizing consumable experiences and disposable consumption over deep-rooted social cohesion. Researchers frequently describe the displacement of \"old\" residents not just as an economic trade-off but as an erasure of the neighborhood's unique social fabric and local knowledge bases. Ultimately, studies often argue that true neighborhood quality of life requires integrating investment strategies with robust protections for existing community ties.",
      "source": "target",
